(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the molecular mechanism of Alternative Splicing regulation by SR proteins, hnRNPs, and exonic/intronic splicing enhancers \(ESEs/ISEs\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Molecular Regulation of Alternative Pre-mRNA Splicing:) Tj
T*
(   1. Biological Significance:) Tj
T*
(   Alternative splicing allows a single pre-mRNA transcript to generate multiple distinct mature) Tj
T*
(   mRNA isoforms encoding proteins with differing functional domains, cellular localizations, or) Tj
T*
(   regulatory affinities. Over 95% of multiexon human genes undergo alternative splicing,) Tj
T*
(   dramatically expanding proteomic diversity.) Tj
T*
(   2. Cis-Acting Regulatory RNA Elements:) Tj
T*
(   In addition to standard splice sites, pre-mRNAs contain short degenerate sequence motifs) Tj
T*
(   categorized into four regulatory classes:) Tj
T*
(   - Exonic Splicing Enhancers \(ESEs\): Stimulate inclusion of the exon in which they reside.) Tj
T*
(   - Intronic Splicing Enhancers \(ISEs\): Stimulate recognition of adjacent splice sites from) Tj
T*
(   within the intron.) Tj
T*
(   - Exonic Splicing Silencers \(ESSs\): Inhibit inclusion of the host exon.) Tj
T*
(   - Intronic Splicing Silencers \(ISSs\): Repress splicing of nearby splice sites.) Tj
T*
(   3. Trans-Acting Splicing Factors:) Tj
T*
(   - SR Proteins \(Serine/Arginine-Rich Family, e.g., SRSF1 / SF2/ASF, SRSF2\):) Tj
T*
(   * Structural Organization: Possess one or two N-terminal RNA Recognition Motifs \(RRMs\) that) Tj
T*
(   bind sequence-specifically to ESE motifs, and a C-terminal RS domain rich in alternating) Tj
T*
