(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the chemical and physical basis of DNA Denaturation \(Melting\), Hyperchromicity, and the factors determining Melting Temperature \(Tm\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Thermodynamics and Biophysics of DNA Denaturation \(Melting\):) Tj
T*
(   1. The Denaturation Transition:) Tj
T*
(   DNA denaturation is the thermal or chemical dissociation of the double-stranded, base-paired) Tj
T*
(   duplex into two separate, disordered single strands, accompanied by loss of base-stacking) Tj
T*
(   interactions and hydrogen bonds.) Tj
T*
(   2. The Hyperchromic Effect:) Tj
T*
(   - In native double-stranded B-DNA, the aromatic purine and pyrimidine rings are tightly stacked) Tj
T*
(   parallel to each other at a distance of 0.34 nm. This close parallel orbital overlap constrains) Tj
T*
(   electronic transitions, quenching the absorption of ultraviolet light at 260 nm) Tj
T*
(   \(hypochromicity\).) Tj
T*
(   - Upon thermal denaturation, the strands uncoil into random coils; the bases unstack and rotate) Tj
T*
(   freely in solution, exposing their pi-electron systems.) Tj
T*
(   - This causes an immediate ~30% to 40% increase in optical absorbance at 260 nm \(Hyperchromic) Tj
T*
(   Shift / Hyperchromicity\).) Tj
T*
(   3. The Melting Temperature \(Tm\):) Tj
T*
(   Tm is defined operationally as the midpoint temperature of the cooperative thermal denaturation) Tj
T*
(   curve, where exactly 50% of the DNA molecules exist as native double-stranded duplex and 50% as) Tj
T*
(   denatured single strands.) Tj
T*
(   4. Physicochemical Determinants of Tm:) Tj
T*
(   - G-C Content: G-C pairs are linked by three hydrogen bonds, whereas A-T pairs are linked by) Tj
T*
(   only two. More importantly, G-C base-stacking interactions have substantially higher enthalpy.) Tj
T*
